document TeamsAndHomes: "Teams And Homes"
    "Load this when an author is sharing a role across teams, naming members, or porting a Markdown home. Roles carry the craft. Teams say where agents live. Values supply the words that differ."
    section values: "One Role, Each Home's Words"
        "In example 173, one QA role reads bet or feature from the concrete agent's values. Use the agent late root in shared prose. The compiler fills it after inheritance. It lists all missing values at once and names value cycles. Code stays literal. Skills, emitted documents, and machine output schemas have no home values."
        "The agent's own values win. Next come its team and outer teams by folder, then parent agent defaults. A value may read another value or an ordinary checked ref. That ref keeps its source module. A sentence for one team belongs in an override; values add no logic."
    section teams: "Folders Hold Shared Doctrine"
        "Examples 174 and 175 show team, home, and member. A team holds its own role and slots. That text stays out of each member's own render. The runtime reads the folder above, so the team lesson lands once. Paths form the tree; no declaration names an outer team."
        "A member is Team.key. Its key changes underscores to hyphens in the folder name. Team.key also works in checked refs, routes, and rule targets. A member may take its role from an abstract agent, a concrete agent, or another member."
        "Teams and members keep inherited items not named in their bodies. The role comes first, then own items, then the remaining craft. An explicit inherit * places the craft where it stands. Ordinary agent children still account for inherited items in full."
    section assembly: "Reuse A Team's Member List"
        "Example 176 stamps one abstract team's members into several concrete teams. Add a new member, drop one with a reason, or replace one whole with override member. The inherited members keep their order and come before new members."
        "The compiler supplies key, title, home, and folder facts. A team also supplies members and others as readable title lists. Do not set these names in values. A team file has no others, and an agent outside a team has no home or folder."
    section roster: "Show Who Makes Each Call"
        "Example 177 uses roster to make a table from direct members. Each column has a header and a cell template that reads agent values. The agent root means that row's member. A missing cell value skips that member's row. Static rows follow and fill every column. A roster needs a team."
    section markdown: "Port One Markdown File First"
        "Example 179 declares an abstract agent and a workflow from Markdown paths. Text before the first level-two heading becomes the role or preamble. Level-two and level-three headings become keys; deeper headings stay text. Fenced headings stay literal."
        "A heading key is lower case, with runs of punctuation and spaces changed to underscores. Duplicate sibling keys fail with both line numbers. These sections take drop, override, dotted patches, and checked title refs. Text stays verbatim, including double braces. A runtime import line fails; declare that file too and compose it."

    section companions: "Keep The Idea Close And Longer Cases Nearby"
        "Examples 181 and 182 keep the short idea in the home and route details to a companion. A team or member owns DETAILS.md. A concrete agent puts it beside its emitted file. An abstract agent, abstract team, workflow, or document owns one shared _details file."
        "The owner is where the text was written. A shared companion keeps that owner's headings and order even when readers reorder or retitle them. It cannot read one agent's or team's values. Move such a case into the home that owns those words."
        "A named details block gets a heading. A bare details block stays under its section. Paragraphs can hold details too. One pointer line links the home's companions in first-use order. Skills, skill packages, input or output bodies, output structures, and emitted documents cannot use details."
    section budgets: "Judge What The Runtime Loads"
        "Example 183 counts externals first, then the runtime file at each folder level down to the home. Details never count. Claude counts UTF-16 units; Codex counts UTF-8 bytes. A budget takes a runtime name and a positive integer no larger than the runtime's limit."
        "The nearest budget wins: the home, its team, outer teams, then the runtime limit. Check fails an over-budget home unless over is warn. Build only warns, so a launch can still start. At warn_at of the limit, W306 gives the author an early signal."
        "Run doctrine load to see all team and member homes by headroom. Select one home for its file order, biggest source parts, and drop reasons. Sizes include externals and each loaded level. Details stay separate. Nested source entries never count text twice."
